**Proof.**

First, we note that all $b_k$ are positive. Indeed, $b_1 \geq a_1 > 0$ gives $b_1 > 0$; then $b_1 b_2 \geq a_1 a_2 > 0$ with $b_1 > 0$ gives $b_2 > 0$; continuing inductively, $b_k > 0$ for all $k$.

**Setup.** Define the partial products
$$A_k = a_1 a_2 \cdots a_k, \qquad B_k = b_1 b_2 \cdots b_k \qquad (k=1,\dots,n),$$
and set $A_0 = B_0 = 1$. The hypothesis gives $B_k \geq A_k > 0$ for all $k$. Define
$$c_k = \frac{B_k}{A_k} \geq 1, \qquad c_0 = 1.$$
Since $b_k = \dfrac{B_k}{B_{k-1}} = \dfrac{A_k \, c_k}{A_{k-1}\, c_{k-1}} = a_k \,\dfrac{c_k}{c_{k-1}}$, we have
$$\sum_{k=1}^n b_k = \sum_{k=1}^n a_k \,\frac{c_k}{c_{k-1}}.$$

**Decomposition of $a_k$.** Since $a_1 \geq a_2 \geq \cdots \geq a_n > 0$, set $a_{n+1}=0$ and define
$$d_j = a_j - a_{j+1} \geq 0 \qquad (j=1,\dots,n).$$
Then $a_k = \sum_{j=k}^{n} d_j$ for each $k$ (telescoping: $\sum_{j=k}^n (a_j - a_{j+1}) = a_k - a_{n+1} = a_k$).

**Rewrite the sums.** Substituting and exchanging the order of summation:
$$\sum_{k=1}^n b_k = \sum_{k=1}^n \left(\sum_{j=k}^n d_j\right) \frac{c_k}{c_{k-1}} = \sum_{j=1}^n d_j \sum_{k=1}^j \frac{c_k}{c_{k-1}}.$$
Likewise
$$\sum_{k=1}^n a_k = \sum_{j=1}^n d_j \sum_{k=1}^j 1 = \sum_{j=1}^n j\, d_j.$$

**Key estimate via AM–GM.** For each $j = 1,\dots,n$, the ratios $\frac{c_k}{c_{k-1}} = \frac{b_k}{a_k} > 0$, so by the AM–GM inequality:
$$\frac{1}{j}\sum_{k=1}^j \frac{c_k}{c_{k-1}} \;\geq\; \left(\prod_{k=1}^j \frac{c_k}{c_{k-1}}\right)^{\!1/j} = \left(\frac{c_j}{c_0}\right)^{\!1/j} = c_j^{\,1/j}.$$
Since $c_j \geq 1$, we have $c_j^{\,1/j} \geq 1$, and therefore
$$\sum_{k=1}^j \frac{c_k}{c_{k-1}} \;\geq\; j \qquad (j=1,\dots,n).$$

**Conclusion.** Since every $d_j \geq 0$,
$$\sum_{k=1}^n b_k - \sum_{k=1}^n a_k = \sum_{j=1}^n d_j\!\left(\sum_{k=1}^j \frac{c_k}{c_{k-1}} - j\right) \;\geq\; 0.$$
Hence $b_1 + b_2 + \cdots + b_n \geq a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n$. $\blacksquare$

